joining me now is khalid hussaini the  legendary author of the kite runner and  two other acclaimed novels set in  afghanistan that have introduced  americans to what life was like in his  home country before it was terrorized by  taliban warlords just listen to his  description of the dreams of a young  girl in the novel a thousand splendid  sons  it was easy to tell mullah fazola things  that murriam didn't dare tell nana one  day as they were walking mariam told him  that she wished she would be allowed to  go to school i mean a real school  akansaheb like in a classroom like my  father's other kids mullah fazola  stopped the week before bibi joe had  brought news that jaleel's daughter said  naheed were going to the mary school for  girls in herat since then thoughts of  classrooms and teachers had rattled  around in murriam's head images of  notebooks with lined pages  columns of numbers and pens that made  dark heavy marks she pictured herself in  a classroom with other girls her age  mariam longed to place a ruler on a page  and draw important looking lines is that  what you want maleficent said looking at  her with his soft watery eyes his hands  behind his stooping back the shadow of  his turban falling on a patch of  bristling buttercups  yes  hussaini in my view has done more than  any other person to portray the unique  diversity and reality of human beings in  afghanistan he grew up in kabul before  moving to the u.s in 1980 when he and  his family were granted political asylum  he's also the founder of the khalid  husseini foundation an organization  providing humanitarian assistance in  afghanistan  thanks again for speaking with me  tonight  last week when you and i spoke you told  me how your heart was broken by what's  been happening in afghanistan i wonder  how do you feel today any less depressed  any more optimistic about the future at  all  honestly media  try to look for a positive angle moving  forward because that's something i've  always looked for i always  try to find a sliver of hope but right  now  things are pretty bleak  i'm watching the situation in the  airport  and while that's obviously very very  important that's happening right now i'm  also thinking long term about the future  of the country  and particularly about women and girls  you read the excerpt from from my book  and that speaks to one of my essential  concerns what will happen to women what  will happen to girls what will happen in  education what will happen to all the  rights that they have gained over the  last 20 years through blood sweat and  tears  yeah and  this is the big issue we talk about  afghanistan's mariams we just mentioned  from a thousand splendid sons a lot of  the people who didn't want to pull out  of afghanistan rightly or wrongly they  cited the progress made  over the last 20 years because it's easy  and it's right to highlight the costs of  the war the people who died and we've  done that extensively on this show i'm  someone who openly supported uh the u.s  withdrawal from afghanistan but it would  be wrong to deny the wins the progress  that was made especially for women and  girls and that undeniably is going to go  backwards even though this new  supposedly moderate taliban is claiming  that they will respect women's rights  they say  look when the taliban fled  in 2001 after the u.s camp campaign  began  there were roughly 900 000 children in  school in the entire country a nation of  some 30 plus million people  and they were  all of them boys  by 2020 over 9 million children were in  school and almost 40 percent of them  were girls  that is  very difficult to dismiss  millions of girls returned to school  they had hopes of  education of securing a future for  themselves and of becoming  you know  relevant players in the rebuilding of  this country  that is entirely up in the air now and  frankly at the mercy of the taliban  unless as i hope the international  community beginning with the us and her  partners bring legitimate diplomatic  pressure on the taliban to not abuse  the essential human rights of women and  girls and allow them access to education  to work and and and and other and  systems of justice  yes  and  i mean that's all that's left now is  that kind of international pressure and  pressure from some of our allies who do  have leverage over the taliban the  taliban is warning today of consequences  if the u.s delays its withdrawal of  troops beyond next tuesday's august 31st  deadline biden said today the us is on  track but he's also making contingency  plans are you filled with confidence  when you hear the president speak about  the timeline i mean on the one hand he's  been criticized for  you know botching the evacuation on the  other hand as he pointed out today  70 000 people evacuated just since  august 14th isn't a small amount of  people that is an achievement  yeah look i  i like president biden i voted for him  and giving the opponent i i would again  in a heartbeat  that said  he has said multiple times that we're  not going to leave until every last  american citizen is evacuated from  afghanistan and i would like to hear the  same about the u.s allies the afghan  afghans who risked their lives in  supporting and in many cases protecting  u.s troops  i'm thinking about those interpreters  and their families who are now  who believe i believe quite justifiably  that they are targets against the  taliban  i believe there is a moral mandate to  make sure that our partners the people  who risk their lives who sacrifice who  risk the lives of their families to  align themselves with u.s objectives are  protected and evacuated and god forbid  in the coming days and weeks if images  circulate of  afghan allies of the united states being  executed by the taliban that will be a  blood state would be very difficult to  wash off  it's a very good point and we do have a  moral and a strategic obligation to  those people  after 20 years of occupying that country  uh khalid what are you hearing from your  contacts on the ground in afghanistan  from your friends from your colleagues  from family members about what the  situation is like now especially outside  of kabul  well i have family in western  afghanistan and they're  frankly scared they're anxious they're  uncertain about what's going to happen  the us's withdrawal will be complete by  august 31st and then the taliban will  essentially take over and nobody knows  what the future holds for afghanistan  nobody knows what kind of government the  taliban intent to run they've been  saying some of the right things they've  been saying they will not abuse afghan  civilians that they will not abuse women  they will respect human rights uh but i  think quite rightly quite justifiably  millions of afghans uh are skeptical  about those words they remember the last  time the taliban ran the country they  remembered the ruthless ways in which  the brutalized and terrorized afghans  for the last 20 years and i think  they're quite rightly skeptical of what  the taliban are saying that is certainly  what i'm hearing from from family  members that i've spoken to and also  colleagues within kabul  khalid a new poll shows americans by  more than two to one say the war in  afghanistan was not worth it three out  of four predict the taliban-led country  will again become a haven for terrorists  looking to target the united states  president joe biden's approval rating  has taken a hit too yet only seven  percent of those surveyed believe he's  the president most at fault for what has  happened in afghanistan nearly two in  three in my view rightly blame the  commander-in-chief who started it all  george w bush i wonder though as an  afghan-american do you believe that the  conflict was worth it looking back at  the entire 20-year period  look i think it's a really a legitimate  question maybe and and then that one  that afghans are asking themselves  it is undeniable  that in the last 20 years significant  progress was made the challenges the  failures of the last 20 years have been  well documented  and i don't want to reiterate those  because your viewers are probably very  familiar with them from the failure to  form a legitimate government to the drug  trade to the inability to bring security  under control  but look millions of children went back  to school  women returned to the government  they became their return to the  workforce uh millions of afghans enjoyed  personal freedoms that would have been  unthinkable under the taliban millions  of young afghans and this is a young  nation now interested in social justice  interested in 21st century ideas  logged on and and connected with the  outside world through social media  so the country changed  quite a lot and the country that the  taliban fled from in 2001 is not the one  that they've conquered again in in 20  and 21. so while i think for your  average viewer afghanistan is synonymous  with military disaster with  the taliban with the drug trade the  reality is for average people at least  in urban centers in afghanistan uh the  life had changed quite  quite significantly  there was reporting khalid that joe  biden's administration didn't get all  their special immigrant visa process  sorted out earlier because they were  worried about a backlash from bringing  in thousands of afghan asylum seekers  and yet the polls show now a majority of  americans support us doing that  especially bringing in interpreters you  have thousands of afghans now  successfully evacuated you have  companies like airbnb today offering  free accommodation to thousands of  afghan refugees does that make you feel  hopeful about humanity or should we be  worried about the inevitable political  backlash to all of this from the  nativist anti-immigrant right in this  country  well we can expect that narrative to  continue but the fact is look i came to  the united states as an asylum seeker  meaning that i have not only personal  kinship with displaced people but i have  personal kinship and a personal  relationship with the moral and  political authority that this country  has traditionally brought to this issue  you know historically the united states  has been a leader  in in welcoming and protecting victims  of war and persecution and it's high  time that the united states step up  again and amp up its supports for afghan  refugees not only those who risk their  lives for u.s initiatives and u.s  objectives but also the millions of  others who work to secure their  communities and bought into the american  objectives and initiatives who are now  left behind after the americans left and  are wondering what is going to happen  with them  i'm so glad you mentioned that because a  lot of the debate has just been about  interpreters who of course did risk  their lives but a lot of other afghans  are also a threat and need our support  one last question for you khalid you  have probably done more than most in my  view to portray afghans to the world  as real people not as props in a cold  war or in a war on terror narrative for  the west and i wonder how should we  think about afghans and afghanistan now  with the taliban back in charge how do  we avoid generalizing about this country  that is so rich in its diversity it's  culture it's history and yes it's art  too you yourself are a proud afghan  artist a novelist a storyteller how do  we do that  that's been one of the things i've  really grappled with for not just in  this moment and and for for a long time  you know for a lot of people afghanistan  is synonymous with conflict with war and  persecution and displacement but the  fact is and i was just speaking to an  italian reporter earlier today who told  me that she's been to afghanistan three  times and there's no place on earth that  left an imprint on the heart like  afghanistan did it's a beautiful country  filled with beautiful people who have  poetry in their soul who are humble who  are hospitable and who are kind they  don't deserve the four years of violence  and persecution and cruelties that  they've endured and now is not the time  to turn our back to afghanistan and its  beautiful people i hope  america and her partners  stand up for americans and i ask your  readers i'm sorry your audience ask your  your audience to reach out to the  leaders  and encourage them with continued  support for afghanistan and particularly  for those who are going to be displaced  who are going to be looking for safe  territory keep our borders open and  forth for afghan refugees and continue  supporting those afghans who are fleeing  this brutal regime and are looking for  safety  we can only hope and pray khalid  hosseini thank you so much for your time  tonight appreciate it  that was the author of the kite runner  and a thousand splendid sons and i  am so delighted and privileged that we  were able to bring him back on the show  to talk again to talk about a beautiful  country with beautiful people and poetry  in their soul because  in the coming days you're going to see a  lot of images out of afghanistan of  people with guns and people with weapons  and let's not think about afghanistan  only in that way let's think about it in  the way that the likes of khaled  hosseini have portrayed it for us  hi i'm mehdi hussain thanks for checking  out our channel on youtube you can see  more of the mehdi hassan show by  clicking on any of the videos on this  screen and make sure you subscribe below  to stay up to date on the day's biggest  stories thank you for watching

